INT. MENDOZA-FAIRCLOUGH FAMILY KITCHEN - DUSK

A narrow galley kitchen in a house built for a bigger family. Yellowed curtains, a fridge covered in magnets from places no one has visited in years. On the counter, a cake with one candle already melted.

GRANDMOTHER EULALIA MENDOZA (70s, cardigan buttoned wrong) arranges forks into a fan. She stops, straightens them, arranges them again.

Her son, HECTOR FAIRCLOUGH-MENDOZA (40s, shirt sleeves rolled, a ring of sweat at the collar) stands at the stove, stirring a pot that has long since stopped needing it. His daughter, NOEMI (16, headphones round her neck, thumbnail polish chipped to nubs), sits at the table peeling a label off a jar in one long unbroken strip. At the end of the table, TEODORO (9, in a school tie knotted too tight) draws a small, careful circle on a napkin, and then another, and then another.

HECTOR
(to the room)
Dinner in five.

No one answers. Eulalia moves a fork a quarter-inch.

NOEMI
Is Dad coming?

HECTOR
(to the pot)
He said he'd try.

EULALIA
(to herself)
He always says that.

Teodoro finishes a circle. He draws a line connecting it to the others.

NOEMI
Teo, what is that?

TEODORO
(not looking up)
It's the table.

NOEMI
We're five. There's four chairs.

TEODORO
I know.

The label tears. Noemi drops it and leaves the jar on the table. Hector turns off the burner and the pot ticks as it cools. Eulalia sets down the last fork, and all of them, one by one, look at the empty chair.

NOEMI (CONT'D)
(to everyone, tight)
Can we please just eat.

HECTOR
Noemi --

NOEMI
I said, can we please just eat!

She shoves her chair back. It scrapes. Eulalia flinches. Teodoro does not.
